### Safe H-Bridge State Machine with Dead-Time Protection

The following C++ sketch models an H-Bridge control architecture. Notice how the `allSwitchesOff()` function enforces a hardware **dead-time delay** between state transitions, guaranteeing that cross-conduction shoot-through is mathematically impossible.

```cpp
// 4-Switch Discrete H-Bridge Controller
// Enforces dead-time protection to prevent shoot-through short circuits
const int legA_High = 2; // Q1 (Top-Left)
const int legA_Low  = 3; // Q2 (Bottom-Left)
const int legB_High = 4; // Q3 (Top-Right)
const int legB_Low  = 5; // Q4 (Bottom-Right)

void setup() {
  pinMode(legA_High, OUTPUT);
  pinMode(legA_Low,  OUTPUT);
  pinMode(legB_High, OUTPUT);
  pinMode(legB_Low,  OUTPUT);

  // Initial safety state: all switches open
  allSwitchesOff();

  Serial.begin(9600);
  Serial.println(F("--- H-Bridge Controller with Dead-Time Interlock ---"));
}

void allSwitchesOff() {
  // Disengage all 4 switches
  digitalWrite(legA_High, LOW);
  digitalWrite(legA_Low,  LOW);
  digitalWrite(legB_High, LOW);
  digitalWrite(legB_Low,  LOW);
  // Crucial dead-time buffer: ensure opening transistors fully turn off
  delayMicroseconds(50);
}

void driveForward() {
  allSwitchesOff(); // Prevent shoot-through before switching!
  digitalWrite(legA_High, HIGH); // Q1 ON
  digitalWrite(legB_Low,  HIGH); // Q4 ON
  Serial.println(F("STATE: Forward (Q1 + Q4 ON)"));
}

void driveReverse() {
  allSwitchesOff(); // Prevent shoot-through before switching!
  digitalWrite(legB_High, HIGH); // Q3 ON
  digitalWrite(legA_Low,  HIGH); // Q2 ON
  Serial.println(F("STATE: Reverse (Q3 + Q2 ON)"));
}

void dynamicBrake() {
  allSwitchesOff();
  digitalWrite(legA_Low, HIGH); // Q2 ON
  digitalWrite(legB_Low, HIGH); // Q4 ON (Both terminals shorted to GND)
  Serial.println(F("STATE: Dynamic Regenerative Brake Active"));
}

void loop() {
  driveForward();
  delay(2000);

  dynamicBrake();
  delay(1000);

  driveReverse();
  delay(2000);

  dynamicBrake();
  delay(1000);
}
```

### Code Breakdown:
- `allSwitchesOff()`: Shuts down all four switches and inserts a `delayMicroseconds(50)` dead-time window before closing opposite switch pairs.
- `driveForward()` & `driveReverse()`: Activates diagonally opposite switch pairs ($Q_1 + Q_4$ vs $Q_3 + Q_2$) to reverse current flow across the motor.
- `dynamicBrake()`: Closes both bottom switches ($Q_2 + Q_4$), tying both motor terminals to ground so the motor acts as an electromagnetic brake.
